---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import TokenizeEmbedDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/TokenizeEmbedDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "00-foundations";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<BaseLayout title="00 · Foundations" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Before touching any cloud service, get the vocabulary straight. Every later module assumes you know these
        five ideas cold.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Tokens</h2>
    <p>
      LLMs read <strong>tokens</strong> — sub-word chunks — not characters or words. Pricing, context limits, and
      latency are all measured in tokens. Rule of thumb: 1 token ≈ 4 characters ≈ ¾ of a word.
    </p>

    <h2>Embeddings &amp; vector similarity</h2>
    <p>
      An embedding model maps text to a vector so similar meanings land close together, measured by cosine similarity
      or dot product — the mechanism that lets retrieval find "docs about X" without exact keyword matches.
    </p>

    <TokenizeEmbedDemo />

    <h2>Context windows</h2>
    <p>
      The max tokens (input + output) one model call can hold. Everything retrieved has to fit alongside the system
      prompt, history, and question — why chunking and retrieval quality (Module 35) matter more as context grows.
    </p>

    <h2>RAG vs. fine-tuning vs. long-context</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Three ways to get a model to "know" your data:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">RAG</span>
        <p>Retrieve relevant chunks at query time, put them in the prompt.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">FINE-TUNING</span>
        <p>Bake knowledge/behavior into the model's own weights.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">LONG-CONTEXT</span>
        <p>Paste everything into a huge context window.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>This curriculum is RAG-first — cheapest to keep fresh, swap the index, not the model.</p>

    <h2>RAG vs. agents: one more foundational fork</h2>
    <p>
      The three options above assume one question, one answer. Some tasks aren't: "find X, compare it against last
      quarter, draft a summary" needs several steps. That's an <strong>agent</strong> (Module 85) — a loop that
      plans, acts, and observes across tool calls, often including the RAG call itself. Default answer until proven
      otherwise: RAG — simpler, cheaper, faster than an agent loop on a task that didn't need one.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Confusing "similar embedding" with "relevant answer" — similarity is necessary, not sufficient.</li>
      <li>Assuming a bigger context window removes the need for good retrieval — it just raises the cost of a bad one.</li>
      <li>Picking a fine-tune when the real problem is stale or missing data (RAG fixes that; fine-tuning doesn't).</li>
      <li>Reaching for an agent loop by default — most questions are a single RAG lookup, and an agent loop only earns its extra latency and cost on genuinely multi-step tasks.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</BaseLayout>
